<meta type=article>
<title | Ordinary Least Squares in One Page>
<subtitle | A Single-Article Live Example>
<author | The Enscribe Team>
</meta>

<config ref-prefix-eqn="Eq." />

<section | The model>

Given paired observations <$ (x_i, y_i) $> for <$ i = 1, \dots, n $>, the
simple linear model predicts <$ \hat{y}_i = m x_i + b $>. The fit is judged by
the residual sum of squares — the total squared vertical distance between the
data and the line:

<$$ #eqn:rss | S(m, b) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \left( y_i - m x_i - b \right)^2 $$>

Ordinary least squares chooses the slope <$ m $> and intercept <$ b $> that
make <ref @eqn:rss> as small as possible.

<section | The normal equations>

Setting the partial derivatives of <ref @eqn:rss> to zero and solving gives the
closed-form minimizer:

<$$ #eqn:slope | m = \frac{\sum_i (x_i - \bar{x})(y_i - \bar{y})}{\sum_i (x_i - \bar{x})^2} $$>

with the intercept following from the means, <$ b = \bar{y} - m \bar{x} $>.
Because <ref @eqn:rss> is convex in both parameters, the solution in
<ref @eqn:slope> is the unique global minimum — no iteration required.

<section | Why one render suffices>

This whole document is a single unit: the cross-references in
<ref @eqn:slope> and the section above resolve in one pass, with no chapters to
route between. That is exactly what the live shell does in the browser — render
the one article, and (with <code | ?edit>) let you rewrite the source and watch
it re-render.
